{"skill":{"slug":"telegram-compose","displayName":"Telegram Compose"},"version":{"version":"1.0.4","createdAt":1770935193951,"changelog":"**Summary:**  \nThis update requires callers to explicitly specify the Telegram bot account for all deliveries; auto-selection of accounts is no longer allowed.\n\n- The bot account name (e.g., \"main\") must now be provided by the caller for every message; the skill will not auto-select or iterate accounts.\n- Session/task templates and documentation updated to require explicit `Bot account: <account_name>` specification.\n- Credentials section and all usage examples revised to validate and use only the specified account.\n- Added `metadata` with OS, binary, network, and credential requirements.\n- Improved error handling and validation message if account is missing or invalid.\n- No runtime/code file changes—documentation only.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":9617,"sha256":"6f134a0d0e3028a58180b06bb6d7aa33d4d25380a409e4931cbc1efb66de3a17","contentType":null},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2170,"sha256":"7e5adcde5b9641dc3e34614705a9ebce2ddcb86c3fbd3e1a9f74e6c1b06b509f","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789022594154,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"5d7deba83c75b39004b0f8576f7550ca0954c8bfc93973bb31eabc326c2fa3b7","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/5d7deba83c75b39004b0f8576f7550ca0954c8bfc93973bb31eabc326c2fa3b7","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: telegram-compose\nVersion: 1.0.4\n\nThe skill contains a critical shell injection vulnerability in `SKILL.md`. The `$ACCOUNT` variable, which is provided by the caller, is directly interpolated into a `jq` command (`jq -r \".channels.telegram.accounts.$ACCOUNT.botToken\" \"$CONFIG\"`) without sanitization. This allows for arbitrary command execution if a malicious string is passed as `$ACCOUNT` (e.g., `foo\"; rm -rf / #`). While the skill's instructions aim for secure usage and there's no evidence of intentional malicious behavior like unauthorized data exfiltration or persistence, this RCE vulnerability makes the skill highly risky.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160179145},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":30,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":5,"checkedAt":1789022594154},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill sends Telegram messages as advertised, but it needs Review because it can automatically transmit content using a local bot token and may expose messages or credentials through weak routing and token handling.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Reading the OpenClaw bot token and sending to Telegram are purpose-aligned, but the direct curl pattern places the bot token in the command-line URL and destinations are task-provided without documented allowlisting.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The artifact is a Markdown skill with no installer, package downloads, background services, or bundled executable files; it declares jq, curl, OpenClaw config, exec, Read, and Telegram network use.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The auto-invocation rule is broad for substantive Telegram output, and the skill does not document sensitivity checks or explicit confirmation before sending externally; it also retries invalid topic sends into the General topic.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No persistence, privilege escalation, tool hijacking, memory writes, or cross-session modification behavior was found.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The stated purpose matches the capability: format rich Telegram messages and send them to api.telegram.org using a caller-specified bot account, chat ID, and optional thread ID.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable with this skill automatically sending Telegram-targeted substantive messages through a bot token stored in OpenClaw config. Keep the bot limited to intended chats, avoid sending secrets or sensitive reports through it, and treat invalid topic fallback and command-line token exposure as issues to fix before high-sensitivity use.","findings":"[SQP-1] expected: The artifact supports the concern that the skill auto-spawns for substantive Telegram replies; this is disclosed and limited to Telegram output, but it lacks clear sensitivity or confirmation gates before external delivery.\n[E1] expected: External transmission to api.telegram.org is the core disclosed purpose, so this is not covert exfiltration by itself; the concern remains because content and caller-provided destinations leave the local trust boundary.\n[T09] unexpected: The Telegram bot token is interpolated into the curl URL, which can expose the credential through process arguments or logs; this implementation detail is not necessary to the user-facing purpose.\n[T09] unexpected: The documented fallback for a missing message thread changes the destination to the General topic, which can disclose content to an unintended Telegram context instead of failing closed.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789022594154}}}}}